AB  - The study explores alternative solutions to current affordability and accessibility challenges facing higher education in Ghana. Three research questions guided the design of the study: 1. What is the current situation concerning accessibility and affordability of higher education in Ghana? 2. What do individuals from various groups, including academics, policymakers, students, and other stakeholders, believe with regard to improving accessibility and affordability? 3. How can we construct a group of these individuals to move forward with implementing the new ideas generated in this process? Qualitative methods were used to collect data. A total of 23 stakeholders, including students, parents, teachers, community leaders, policymakers, professionals, and foreign education experts, engaged in either a focus group discussion or one-on-one interviews, focused on answering 10 questions about finding alternative solutions to the affordability and accessibility challenges. ATLAS software (ATLAS.ti.com, 2017) was used in coding and analyses of the data. Accessibility challenges included failing to pass the standardized West Africa Senior Secondary Examinations (WASSE), lack of funding to assist the majority of the students who come from poor homes, and fewer public universities and the high cost of private universities. Political interference in education and outdated and irrelevant curricula, inherited from colonial masters, were seen to contribute negatively to high graduate unemployment. Lack of corporate partners for internships had led employers to shy away from hiring fresh graduates. The majority of the participants advocated that the Basic Education Certificate Examinations (BECE) be eliminated to enable more students to attain a minimum high school education. They also recommended that curricula at all levels be revised to reflect Ghana's socioeconomic needs and that financial institutions provide government-guaranteed loans to tertiary students. Facility expansion, satellite campuses, and sandwich and distance programs at existing public universities were predicted to grant access to more qualified students. Based on these findings, an action plan for implementation begins with a pilot program and concludes with nationwide adoption. The plan will include study participants, who volunteered to continue with this work. [The dissertation citations contained here are published with the permission of ProQuest LLC. Further reproduction is prohibited without permission. Copies of dissertations may be obtained by Telephone (800) 1-800-521-0600. Web page: http://www.proquest.com/en-US/products/dissertations/individuals.shtml.]
